  "text": "When a lost scroll turned up, the king sent for Huldah. Second Kings twenty two. They are renovating the temple, paying the carpenters and the builders, and during the works Hilkiah the high priest finds the book of the law. Shaphan the scribe reads it to King Josiah, and the king tears his clothes, because he realises how far the nation has drifted from what is written in it. So he needs a ruling. Enquire of the Lord for me, he says, and for the people, and for all Judah, concerning the words of this book that is found. Now think about who is available. He has a high priest. He has a scribe. He has a whole religious establishment on payroll. And they go to Huldah the prophetess, who lived in Jerusalem in the college. Five men, sent by a king, to a woman, for the authoritative reading. And she does not soften it. She sends back judgement on the nation, and mercy for Josiah personally, because his heart was tender. Her reading became national policy. The reformation that follows in the next chapter runs on what she said.",
